cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below, and cite Cook et al. 2019 for the connectome data it depends on."
title: "WormWars 01: C. elegans wiring vs. shuffled and random graphs. A foraging null result."
abstract: >-
  Many parallel 2D worlds on one GPU, in which swarms of small creatures forage and fight. Each
  creature's brain is a continuous-time recurrent network whose wiring is the real C. elegans
  connectome used as a fixed sparsity mask; weights, time constants and biases are evolved.
  Experiment 01 asks whether the real wiring, given one hand-built sensor and motor interface and
  one foraging task, evolves faster or ends up better than degree-preserving shuffles of it or than
  random sparse graphs of the same size. Across 15 independent runs per condition it reaches the
  same final performance and improves measurably more slowly, and the shuffled and random controls
  are indistinguishable from each other. This is a result about one wiring graph with one interface
  on one task, not about biological wiring in general.
type: software
